  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 PM CDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nRemainder of this weekend: As anticipated previously, storms have\ndeveloped along a boundary extending from central AR southeast \nacross northern portions of our forecast area. A favorable \nenvironment for strong/severe microbursts with vertical totals \nnear 27 deg C and DCAPE near 1500 j/kg is supporting few intense \nstorms with marginally severe wind gusts, and the storm threat \nshould continue to the early evening hours. Another round of \nstorms is expected Sunday afternoon a little further south than \ntoday, and we'll need to monitor for additional severe microburst \nthreats. Otherwise, dangerous heat is on the increase and we have\nmoved forward with the issuance of a heat advisory for mainly the\nwestern two thirds of the area where heat indices are forecast to\nexceed 105. /EC/\n\nMonday through Wednesday: With the building ridge, temps will \ntrend upward again, with highs in the triple digits possible \nacross the Delta region. This airmass will remain seasonably \nhumid, though we expect to see increasing daytime mixing as we \nhead into next week with dewpoints dropping into the 60s during \nthe afternoon hours over much of the area. Still with conditions \nbeing so warm, heat indices will approach critical thresholds as \nearly as today across northwest MS/north LA/southeast AR and over \nmuch of the western half of the forecast area by tomorrow. \nAdditional advisories may be warranted for a portion of our area,\nand we continue to highlight the increasing heat stress concerns \nin graphical products. \n\nThursday through next Saturday: By mid to late week, low level \nflow will become increasingly easterly thanks to the orientation \nof the surface high, ushering in a relatively drier airmass. This \nairmass will still support hot days, but with dewpoints dropping \ninto the 60s - perhaps even the 50s as times during daytime mixing\n- it could greatly alleviate heat stress danger through the end \nof the forecast period. Deep layer moisture will begin to increase\nagain late Friday into next Saturday, with returning potential \nfor diurnal convection. /DL/EC/",
